<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salinar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Salinar culture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salinar was a culture of the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, flourishing from about 400 to 200 BCE in the Chicama and Moche valleys. It is known for fortified hilltop settlements, cemeteries and distinctive oxidised pottery, often with stirrup spouts and modelled figures, as well as early gold work. It came after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupisnique">Cupisnique culture</a> and before the Gallinazo and Moche cultures, which developed further its traditions of pottery and irrigation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salinar.jpg" alt="Salinar" width="258" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_cultura_salinar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
